WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.920
Unsavory posts? Taunting? Freezing up?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.760
Some actors fell to the Dark Side&nbsp;
and got fired from one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.960
iconic franchises of all time. Keep watching to&nbsp;
see the Star Wars actors who got the boot. 
 
 

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.280
Currently, Gina Carano is the most&nbsp;
high-profile name in Star Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.680
mostly because she was fired from The&nbsp;
Mandalorian, the most high-profile project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.720
in Star Wars. The former mixed martial artist,&nbsp;
who served as Din Djarin's ally Cara Dune,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.640
was dropped from the show for repeated&nbsp;
impolitic statements on social media.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.080
In the past, she posted statements that have&nbsp;
been viewed as anti-mask and transphobic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.960
but the Tweet that broke the internet's back&nbsp;
compared the current American political climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.960
to Nazi Germany. It's a good rule of thumb&nbsp;
to avoid comparing anything to Nazi Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.000
and Carano violated that maxim in a big&nbsp;
way. She posed the rhetorical question:

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.320
"Jews were beaten in the streets,&nbsp;
not by Nazi soldiers but by their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.480
neighbors ... even by children. Because&nbsp;
history is edited, most people today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.880
don't realize that to get to the point&nbsp;
where Nazi soldiers could easily round up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.720
thousands of Jews, the government first&nbsp;
made their own neighbors hate them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.240
simply for being Jews. How is that any different&nbsp;
from hating someone for their political views?"

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.520
Many readers understandably&nbsp;
took offense at the comment and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.040
demanded she be fired from&nbsp;
her job, and Disney obliged.
 
 

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.400
Even though the Star Wars franchise started&nbsp;
on the big screen, it has found surprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.680
success on the small screen, as well. Before The&nbsp;
Mandalorian was even a glint in Jon Favreau's eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.560
shows like The Clone Wars and Rebels demonstrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.320
the viability of the franchise in TV&nbsp;
form. However, these shows weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.360
without their own controversies — especially&nbsp;
the animated Star Wars series, Resistance.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.480
Star Wars Resistance takes place before&nbsp;
and during The Force Awakens, and includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.760
a few familiar characters, including General Leia&nbsp;
Organa. Originally, Rachel Butera was supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.600
voice this cel-shaded incarnation — not surprising&nbsp;
since Butera previously voiced Leia in Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.880
Battlefront 2. But at the last minute, Butera&nbsp;
was scuttled and replaced by Carolyn Hennesy.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.400
While Disney never made an official statement,&nbsp;
the running theory is that Rachel Butera was fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.520
for posting a video that mocked&nbsp;
Dr. Christine Blasey Ford,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.400
the sexual assault victim who testified against&nbsp;
then-Supreme Court nominee Brett Kavanaugh".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.280
Butera's Twitter account has since been deleted,&nbsp;
increasing the intrigue surrounding this one.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.920
"You're demoted."
Since Star Wars is a sci-fi epic,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.080
spaceship dogfights come with the&nbsp;
territory. As with dogfights on Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.880
the best pilots tend to emerge victorious.&nbsp;
While Luke Skywalker is a skilled pilot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.160
thanks to his connection with the Force, you can't&nbsp;
find a better non-Jedi pilot than Wedge Antilles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.040
Despite the character's legacy, his beginnings&nbsp;
were a little rocky in the casting department.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.280
Initially, Antilles was portrayed by Colin&nbsp;
Higgins. He's the Antilles audiences see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:54.240
the Rebel briefing during Star Wars: Episode IV.&nbsp;
While Higgins had previous acting experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.560
it was all on TV where the cast gets to&nbsp;
rehearse. According to Gerald Home who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.360
played Tessek from Return of the Jedi, the film&nbsp;
didn't have rehearsals, so Higgins struggled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.040
his dialogue — which consisted of one sentence.&nbsp;
Eventually, Higgins spoke a line that was shorter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.960
than intended. After only one day of struggling,&nbsp;
Higgins was let go and replaced by Denis Lawson.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.000
Ironically, when Lawson originally auditioned for&nbsp;
the role of Wedge, he lost it to Colin Higgins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.240
Still, this firing and recasting earned Higgins&nbsp;
the moniker of "Fake Wedge," and eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:31.840
the anthology book From a Certain Point of&nbsp;
View retconned Higgins as Col Takbright.
 
 

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.320
Some of Star Wars' most important&nbsp;
roles have gone to puppeteers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.800
Everyone knows about Mark Hamill, but&nbsp;
without people like the late Kenny Baker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.800
non-human characters such as R2-D2&nbsp;
would have never been possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.360
One of those talented puppeteers was&nbsp;
almost lost thanks to Harrison Ford.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.440
Tim Rose brought numerous characters such as&nbsp;
Salacious B. Crumb and Admiral Ackbar to life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.320
throughout the original Star Wars trilogy. Not&nbsp;
surprising, since Rose studied under the great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:06.240
Jim Henson. But that didn't mean bupkis to Ford,&nbsp;
especially after Rose and the other puppeteers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.520
laughed during the famous scene where Han Solo is&nbsp;
unfrozen from carbonite. After Ford complained,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.360
Rose ribbed Ford's complaint, which everyone&nbsp;
found funny except for Ford. He overheard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.720
the ribbing and said he wouldn't step on stage&nbsp;
until the guy who made fun of him was fired. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.280
an assistant director told Rose that he&nbsp;
was fired. Except it was all for show.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.920
In truth, Rose was allowed to keep working&nbsp;
on the movies, but whenever anyone asked him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.240
he had to say he was the new guy to satisfy&nbsp;
Harrison Ford. For the rest of his Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.600
career, Rose wanted to apologize, but he only&nbsp;
got to see Ford while in full Ackbar costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.360
and it's difficult to talk when you're&nbsp;
trapped in a heavy alien squid puppet.

